0","cdbd40337cafa440","cdbd40337cafa440","cdbd40337cafa440","cdbd40337cafa440","d32bc63254c540f9","d47c68f4e840e2f3","d597f92c31d36d9c","dfb3d433e9750ace","e0f970ef7bdfb496","e32739b2a2d27a18","e349a035cbe849a1","e4510314a57a9076","ea6aec5bbe4d69b7","ed87cd5c620120e8","f2797dcccaf7f15e","f3102f18da0b85ef","f3bb3ab813982cc1","f51775425a3d2ee2","fa53ac6761190ad7","fe3d711944df7801","ff6980837b00b92d","fff67f19b337d92e"],"groups":["604f01afc01ea5d8","33d5afe82e95c179","c5ef4934924761e2","c0bcba8031352a4e","c2704069911df290","0a30dfe51e6b6a57","d49a680a19112ff0","6ab5ac6b6e88d252"],"findings":"## Review of the decision-reader hardening (uncommitted snapshot 29f562ca)\n\nI ran `test/decisions.test.ts` (16/16 pass, exit 0). I did not get a clean full-suite run or a typecheck result, so the rest of the suite is unchecked. The findings below are mostly from reading the code, with the test run and a `trim()` check as the verified parts.\n\n### 1. Mismatches\n\n- **shared-shape (h7/h8): the reader now rejects whitespace-only findings, which the intent doesn't mention.** Before, the reader accepted `finding: \"   \"` (length > 0), and the writer already refused it via `!d.finding?.trim()`. The new test \"a whitespace-only finding is … skipped by the reader\" passes, so the behaviour is real. **Verified** (test run), and the old check is **read** from the diff. The change is sensible, but \"one shape rule\" doesn't say it, and the README doesn't mention it.\n- **shared-shape (h7): the reader now skips any line with `\"complete\": false`.** The old code treated `complete: false` as a plain decision. The README documents this (\"which only a hand-written line has\"), but the group's intent doesn't. **Read.**\n- **bidi-strip / shared-shape: trimming does not catch invisible-only findings.** `isValidDecision` uses `.trim()`, which strips U+FEFF but not U+200B or U+200E. I checked this in node: `['\\u200e','\\u200b','\\ufeff'].map(s => s.trim().length)` gives `[1,1,0]`. So a finding of only `\\u200e` passes both writer and reader, and `plain()` then renders it as a blank finding (`fixed:  `). **Verified** for the trim behaviour; the end-to-end blank output is **read**. A \"non-empty after `plain()`\" check would close this gap, and `plain` is already imported in `src/reviews.ts`.\n\n### 2. Misgraded necessity\n\n- **shared-shape, writer half (h8, the `isValidDecision(d)` call in `checkDecision`) is effectively dead code.** Everything it checks is already enforced just above it: finding is non-empty after trim, outcome is in `OUTCOMES`, and finding and note lengths are capped. For the non-complete path, `isValidDecision` can only fail on the same conditions. **Read.** It's a reasonable guard against future drift, but it's labelled \"required\" when it changes no behaviour today. Relabel it as defensive, or drop it and keep the reader-side call.\n- The remaining groups look correctly graded. `bidi-strip`, `key-order`, and the reader half of `shared-shape` are needed for the task. `docs` and `tests` as supporting are right.\n\n### 3. Removal candidates\n\n- The h8 writer hunk (above). Impact: none today; you lose only the future-drift guard.\n\n### 4. Simplifications\n\n- **key-order: `readDecisions` now runs `JSON.parse` on every note line of 16 KB or less.** The old prefix filter was removed entirely. A plain `l.includes('\"decision\"')` pre-filter would keep the parsing cost down, and it still accepts any key order. Only the over-cap path needs the 64-character position heuristic. **Read.** The junk-line timing test passes (413 ms against a 5000 ms budget), so this is a tidiness point, not a bug.\n\n### 5. Missing risks\n\n- **Unsanitised decision text on the HTML path.** `readDecisions` returns raw text, with no `plain()` call. The terminal path sanitises at print time (`src/commands/reviews.ts` calls `plain` on `finding`, `note`, `by`, `at`). The HTML path (`src/html/data.ts`) takes the raw text. The README and TODO both admit the bidi exception there, but the README sentence \"Text from notes (decisions…) is shown with control characters … removed\" is only true for the terminal. **Read.**\n- **Character coverage is incomplete for \"invisible or reordering\".** Not covered: U+FFF9–FFFB (interlinear annotation anchors), U+FFA0/U+3164/U+115F/U+1160 (blank fillers), U+034F, and U+E0100–E01EF (supplementary variation selectors). Most of these hide or reorder nothing, so this is low severity. **Read.**\n- **Over-cap heuristic.** Hand-written over-cap decision lines with a long `by` (up to 200 characters) will push `\"decision\"` past 64 characters and produce a spurious \"skipped review record\" warning. The author lists this. **Read.**\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
