8","e349a035cbe849a1","e4510314a57a9076","ed87cd5c620120e8","f2797dcccaf7f15e","f3102f18da0b85ef","f51775425a3d2ee2","fa53ac6761190ad7","fe3d711944df7801","ff6980837b00b92d","fff67f19b337d92e"],"groups":["604f01afc01ea5d8","33d5afe82e95c179","c5ef4934924761e2","c0bcba8031352a4e","c2704069911df290"],"findings":"**Review of snapshot dbe36b097b (decision-reader gaps)**\n\nI made no changes in the clone. `tsc --noEmit` exits 0, and `test/decisions.test.ts` passes 15/15, including the new bidi, shape and key-order tests. I did not run the full suite. The findings below are ordered most important first.\n\n**1. Mismatch: `complete: false` lines are now dropped (shared-shape, key-order)**\nThe old reader accepted a non-complete decision with `complete: false`, because it only tested `r.complete === true` and then checked the normal fields. The new `isValidDecision` requires `complete === undefined` on that branch. Nothing the writer produces is affected, but hand-written lines with `\"complete\": false` are now silently skipped. The intent never mentions this, and the README does not describe it. **Verified** by calling `isValidDecision({complete: false, finding: 'x', outcome: 'fixed'})`, which returns `false`. The old behaviour is **read** from the removed lines.\n\n**2. Mismatch with the shared-shape intent: the writer and reader still disagree on normal decisions (shared-shape)**\nThe intent says `isValidDecision` is \"the one shape rule used by the reader and the --complete writer check\". Only the complete branch is shared. Normal decisions still go through `checkDecision` (src/reviews.ts:210), which rejects `finding` when `!finding.trim()`. The reader's non-complete branch only checks `length > 0`, so `finding: \"   \"` is refused by the writer and accepted by the reader. That is the same drift the task set out to close. **Read.** Fix by making the non-complete branch of `isValidDecision` the single check, and having `checkDecision` call it.\n\n**3. Missing risk: the HTML view is not covered (bidi-strip)**\n`plain()` is applied only in terminal output. The HTML path takes decision text from `readDecisions` (src/html/data.ts:130) and commit subjects from `commitSubject` (src/html/data.ts:297) without stripping them. Those values are HTML-escaped, but U+202E-style overrides and zero-width characters still reach the Reviews pane and can reorder or hide text there. The README discloses this (\"but not on the html page\"), so it is a scope gap rather than a hidden mismatch. The task said \"terminal output\", so this is a decision to make explicitly. **Read.**\n\n**4. Trade-off that changes existing output (bidi-strip, misgraded as required)**\nThe bidi-strip group is required by the task. The ZWJ and ZWNJ part is a product choice the author made without being asked. Terminal output for legitimate text changes:\n- `plain('می‌خواهم')` becomes `\"می خواهم\"`.\n- `plain('👨‍👩‍👧')` becomes `\"👨 👩 👧\"`.\n\nBoth are **verified** by running `plain()`. The author disclosed this, but it is a regression on valid content. A cheaper option is to strip U+200B, U+2060-U+2064, U+FEFF, U+180E, tags and the bidi marks, and to keep U+200C and U+200D. The weakness is that ZWJ can still carry hidden text. Either way, the choice should be explicit.\n\n**5. Test gap (tests, supporting)**\nThe `INVISIBLE` list and `INVISIBLE_RE` in the test file omit U+180E, which `plain()` does strip. Removing it from the regex would not fail the test. **Read** from the test file. `plain('a\\u180eb')` returns `\"a b\"` (**verified**), so the behaviour exists but is not guarded.\n\n**6. Over-cap heuristic edge case (key-order)**\n`mayBeDecision` checks only the first 64 characters. An over-cap decision line whose `\"decision\"` key falls after that point is counted as a skipped review record, so the reader prints a \"skipped 1 review record\" warning. The comment documents only the reverse case (an over-cap review line that is treated as silent). **Read**, not run. A simple fix is to fall back to `includes('\"decision\"')` on a bounded prefix, or to document this case in the comment.\n\n**7. Necessity grading**\n- `key-order` and `shared-shape` are correctly graded required. The old `isDecisionLine` rejected any valid line whose first key was not `decision`, so the change is needed. The 64-character check is also needed, because parsing 8 MiB lines would be expensive.\n- `docs` as supporting is accurate, but the README's bidi paragraph repeats the ZWJ trade-off at length. It is accurate, and a shorter version would do.\n- The `tests` group is correctly graded supporting. It is the only evidence for the three groups, and the gap in item 5 is small.\n\n**Removal candidates:** none. Every hunk is either the task or the documentation of it.\n\n**Simplifications**\n- Merge `checkDecision` into `isValidDecision` (item 2). This removes a duplicated rule rather than adding one.\n- If item 4 is decided in favour of keeping ZWJ/ZWNJ, the bidi regex can be split into named groups in the code. The current single long character class with a long comment is hard to scan.\n\n**Missing risks**\n- The `complete: false` change (item 1) is not in the README's compatibility section.\n- The HTML bidi exposure (item 3).\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
